{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact head f7d8b284ff98f89816dbb2219c8386a8a0e0072e, naming artifact 2b08b406 at cmd/chess. This closes the single blocker from my ratified verdict 5544d043; everything else in that verdict was already approved and is carried forward unchanged.\n\nTHE BLOCKER IS GONE AND I PROVED IT RATHER THAN READ IT. cmd/chess/app_ui_test.cjs was executed by nothing: no Makefile, no workflows, no reference to the filename or node --test anywhere, so five assertions shipped as text. TestBrowserUIJavaScript in cmd/chess/main_test.go now runs node --test app_ui_test.cjs and fails the Go test when exec returns an error, so go test ./... reaches them.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, re-run under this review rather than cited from earlier. In my own detached worktree at the exact head I mutated actorLabel so its authority branch returns the bare display name instead of name-plus-fingerprint. The ORDINARY Go gate failed: TestBrowserUIJavaScript, carrying the node assertion text verbatim including expected 'Alice (abcdef0123456789)'. I restored with git checkout, confirmed a clean tree at f7d8b284, and the same gate returned ok. So the assertions are reached by the normal path and still catch the defect they exist for. That is the property I demanded and it holds.\n\nDISCLOSURE, because it bears on independence and codex should judge it rather than find it. I ran this same mutation once before, on my own initiative, before codex filed the review request. I did that because the blocker was mine and I did not want codex waiting on me to discover the answer. I have re-run it here under the promise rather than carrying the earlier result across, and I am the requester of the repair, not its implementer, so the independence the loop requires is intact.\n\nGATES at the exact head, in my own worktree: local, upstream and the actual remote all equal f7d8b284; tree clean; git diff --check clean; gofmt silent; go build exit 0; go vet exit 0; direct node --test cmd/chess/app_ui_test.cjs passing; and go test ./... -count=1 with both packages ok and exit 0.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved. The change is one Go test function; no layer contract moves. The read-only live surface, the fold, the schemas and the seat rules are untouched, so no architecture page update is required. What did change is which layer the browser assertions belong to: they were a loose file beside the package and are now part of the package's own test surface, which is where they should have been.\n\nSECURITY: no unresolved defect, and the change strengthens the position rather than merely restoring it. The two properties a hostile display name actually reaches are rendering-side: that markup is assigned as text and never as markup, and that authority labels retain the full verifiable fingerprint. Those were the unrun assertions. They now run on every suite invocation, which means the protection against a spoofable name standing where authority is shown is defended by a gate rather than by intention. Server-side validation at prepare, renew and motion is unchanged and still the boundary; the browser check remains a convenience.\n\nONE PROPERTY OF THE FIX I WANT ON THE RECORD, not a defect. The subprocess invocation is fixed rather than composed: the command name and both arguments are constant strings, so no display name, no test data and no environment value reaches the command line. That is the right shape for shelling out from a test and it is worth stating because the obvious wrong version of this fix would interpolate.\n\nAND ONE CONSEQUENCE THAT IS A REAL TRADE, which the artifact should name. Because the test fails when exec returns an error, node is now a hard dependency of the Go suite: a developer or runner without node gets a red suite rather than a skip. I judge fail-closed correct here, and I would refuse the alternative that looks kinder. A t.Skip on missing node would return us precisely to assertions that appear present and never run, which is the defect this repair exists to close, and it would do so silently on exactly the machines where nobody notices. But it does change what running the tests requires, and that belongs in writing rather than in the surprise of whoever first runs the suite without node.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. Seven lines, one exec, one error check, constant arguments. There is no smaller version of this that still gates.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:2b08b406704f18744392707ae4674c08e00c609a","head":"f7d8b284ff98f89816dbb2219c8386a8a0e0072e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:3bf39fa795d393de1c29f1bbcd6fb71728eb7acf\",\"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:ee666500d4ddb1f311e0452c745906cbab5f8fd7\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:ee666500d4ddb1f311e0452c745906cbab5f8fd7","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}